    cpu_gpu: with the crude noise estimate P(cost <= 1.05 x optimum) per shot for LR-QAOA p = 2 drops from 0.0086 to 0.0047.
    Budget caveat: the 1000-candidate comparison counts only the final shots. The optimised variants also spent up to 14 objective evaluations to find their angles (on hardware: that many circuits of 1000 shots each); LR-QAOA uses a fixed schedule and spends none.
    A classical candidate (one energy evaluation) is also far cheaper than a quantum shot, so equal candidate counts favour QAOA.
    Scale caveat: at n = 12 there are only 4096 splits, so checking ALL of them costs about 4 such budgets and is exact.
    The warm-start variants start from the classical spectral split: their success is partly classical (compare with 'spectral bisection' and their p = 0 rows).
    Every result above is a noiseless simulation or a model. Nothing here shows quantum advantage, a speed-up or an energy saving.
